WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:13.220
Before you see one single image in the
"Lord of the Rings" film trilogy

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:19.300
you hear a chorus singing in a somewhat unsettling harmonic minor key over black.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:24.700
This is a musical theme that signals the Lothlorian Elfs.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:30.800
It's the theme of Galadriel, who's about to narrate the story of the One Ring in prologue,

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:37.000
but not before the Ring's own theme fades in with the first title.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:48.700
as the prologue ends and an e flat minor chord shifts to an E flat major,

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:53.800
the narration shifts also, from Galadriel to Bilbo, and
the mood changes.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.900
The lighter "Shire Theme" is introduced as Bilbo places us there on a map,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.200
and finally after he places us in time.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:08.229
The "Fellowship" theme plays in full statement as its corresponding subtitle fades in,

00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:11.969
bracketing the opening of the film.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:21.020
Now, if this masterful interplay of themes isn't enough to prove to you already

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.400
that composer Howard Shore is a genius, that's okay.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.200
There's a lot more to say.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.590
The first thing is that these four themes that I've mentioned are actually what's

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:35.719
called Leitmotifs - an operatic
technique popularized by Richard Wagner,

00:01:35.719 --> 00:01:41.800
that fuses musical themes with specific people, events, or places to aid,

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.800
and in some cases, augment the dramatic development of a story.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.900
In order to show how Shore develops his Leitmotifs,

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.100
I'm gonna isolate one: the "Fellowship" theme,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:57.090
which has the most variations and focus on its
development in the "Fellowship of the Ring."

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:01.509
Now, before the "Fellowship" theme can
come together in its full heroic glory,

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:04.200
Shore builds it up with a few variations.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:11.300
The first time we hear it after the title is when Frodo and Sam leave the Shire on the road to Rivendell.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.670
Shore presents a small snippet of the "Shire" theme as foreshadowing of that theme's

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.900
final variation at the end of the film

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.700
and then transitions into a humble statement of the "Fellowship" theme,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.900
using just French horn and cor anglais.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.300
"Remember what Bilbo used to say?

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.500
'It's a dangerous business, Frodo. Going out your door.'"

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.800
This is almost immediately contrasted with another,

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.200
darker variation as Gandalf rides into Isengard to take counsel with Saruman.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.700
Shore adds crashing
cymbals

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.700
and plays the theme off Gandalf's galloping horse,

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.900
and this tense variation
prefigures Saruman's eventual

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.480
betrayal, and the two versions playing
off each other anticipate the eventual

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.800
fracturing of the fellowship later in the film.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.400
The next time we hear the theme,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.629
Frodo and the hobbits have accepted Strider (or Aragorn) as their temporary leader.

00:02:58.629 --> 00:03:02.580
The Fellowship is growing so the brass section is growing too.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.500
We started with one French horn,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.300
now we have three.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.600
But the stately melody of this theme is upset with the timpani drumbeat that adds urgency.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.730
The fellowship Leitmotif doesn't
appear again until after Frodo has been

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:21.900
stabbed by the Ringwraiths. As Arwen
races to get him to Rivendell the

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.960
fellowship theme mixes with a Wraith Theme threatening to overtake it.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.800
The fellowship might dissolve before it even has a chance to fully form.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:39.380
At the middle of the film, the fellowship
finally comes together and Shore echoes

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:44.560
that by presenting a string of three
"Fellowship" theme statements in their full

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.660
orchestration and full glory. First, as
Elrond announces the fellowship.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.280
"Nine companions."

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.020
And third as the fellowship strides
heroically away on their quest.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:06.500
But the second one is the most
interesting to me, just as the Fellowship

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:10.870
is about to depart Shore softly teases
the "Shire" theme before it is

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:15.090
overpowered by the "Fellowship" theme, suggesting that the hobbits priorities have shifted

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:17.790
and they now have to live with the
consequences of that.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:22.300
And it ends with the unique harmony on the second half of the full statement,

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.000
as if to sow a seed of doubt right into the middle of the team as it comes together.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.000
"The Fellowship awaits the Ringbearer."

00:04:34.529 --> 00:04:38.889
We're going to get another exciting full
statement in the Mines of Moria, the only

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:42.399
time that the nine members of the
fellowship will be in action together.

00:04:42.399 --> 00:04:47.379
But the 3/4 time signature of the
Fellowship is quickly swallowed up by

00:04:47.379 --> 00:04:51.979
the 5/4 time signature of the orcs.
And from now on,

00:04:51.979 --> 00:04:57.299
after Gandalf is lost, the "Fellowship"
theme will only appear in fragmented form

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.039
until the very end of the trilogy.

00:05:00.039 --> 00:05:01.240
"Fly, you fools!"

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:05.200
For example, after what's left of the fellowship departs Lothlorien,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.100
the theme can only muster violins
and cor anglais,

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:13.020
and Shore has this already weak variation trampled on as soon as it starts.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.880
Then as they rest on the shore,
the Leitmotif can't find its footing.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:23.480
It wanders away from its full expression as uncertainty and suspicion creep in.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:25.900
"You've so little faith in your own people."

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.780
These insecure variations on the theme 
herald the breaking of the fellowship.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:35.100
First with the death of Boromir,
then with the departure of Frodo and Sam.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:40.300
In fact Shore links these two moments with a severely deflated,

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:46.000
but poignant rendition of the
Leitmotif that fades into silence.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:02.479
Like the fellowship itself, the
"Fellowship" theme builds to its full

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:08.159
formation and then is deconstructed
 until it's only a shell of its former self.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.400
Shore does this with instrumentation, with tempo, and with harmony,

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.539
and he does it for several other Leitmotifs in the trilogy, weaving them back and forth and

00:06:17.539 --> 00:06:23.500
through one another so that the result
is a complex web that entwines with the story.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.869
I think that we hardly grasp the
importance of music in film.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:37.580
It's an invisible layer of pure emotion that guides us, or challenges us, or guides or challenges the drama itself.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.920
In the "Lord of the Rings"
trilogy, Howard Shore gave us perhaps the

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:48.050
most complete and complex exploration
of Leitmotifs in the history of cinema.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:54.710
And the result is a score that's as alive as the world Tolkien gave us.

